---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ConverterHero from '../components/ConverterHero.astro';
import FAQAccordion from '../components/FAQAccordion.astro';
import RelatedPairs from '../components/RelatedPairs.astro';

const site = Astro.site ?? new URL('https://usdtoworld.com');
const pairFaqs = [
  {
    q: 'Why is the Canadian dollar called the "loonie"?',
    a: "The nickname comes from the image of a common loon, a bird native to Canada, featured on the Canadian one-dollar coin since 1987. \"Loonie\" caught on as slang for both the coin and the currency more broadly, and you'll see it used casually in Canadian financial media and everyday conversation alike.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the price of oil really affect USD/CAD?',
    a: "It genuinely does, more than most people expect. Canada is a major oil exporter, and energy makes up a substantial share of its export economy, so the Canadian dollar has historically shown a real correlation with crude oil prices — rising oil prices tend to support the loonie, while falling oil prices tend to weigh on it, on top of whatever the usual interest-rate-driven dynamics are doing.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Is 1 USD always worth more than 1 CAD?',
    a: "In recent decades, yes, the US dollar has generally traded above the Canadian dollar, though the gap has varied — sometimes the loonie has come close to parity, and even briefly exceeded the US dollar in value in the mid-2000s and again around 2011. It's not a fixed or guaranteed relationship, so check the live rate for today's actual figure.",
  },
];

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: site.toString() },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'USD to CAD', item: new URL('/usd-to-cad', site).toString() },
  ],
};
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: pairFaqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
};
---
<Layout
  title="USD to CAD Converter — Live Dollar to Canadian Dollar Rate | UsdtoWorld"
  description="Convert US Dollars to Canadian Dollars with a live exchange rate. Get 1 USD to CAD, 100 USD to CAD, and 1000 USD to CAD instantly, plus why oil prices matter."
  path="/usd-to-cad"
  schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema]}
  speakableSelectors={['h1', '.hero-sub']}
>
  <div class="container page-hero">
    <Breadcrumbs trail={[{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'USD to CAD', href: '/usd-to-cad' }]} />
  </div>

  <ConverterHero
    title="USD to CAD Converter (Live Exchange Rate)"
    subtitle="Convert US Dollars to Canadian Dollars instantly with a live market rate — useful for cross-border shopping, travel, and business between two deeply linked economies."
    defaultFrom="USD"
    defaultTo="CAD"
  />

  <section class="section editorial">
    <div class="container editorial-body prose">
      <p class="editorial-byline">Reviewed by the <a href="/about#who-writes-and-reviews-this">UsdtoWorld editorial team</a> · Sourced from live market rate data</p>
      <h2>Two economies that trade constantly</h2>
      <p>
        The US and Canada share the world's longest border and one of the biggest trading relationships
        anywhere. That makes USD/CAD an everyday rate for people on both sides — not just traders. Common uses
        include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Cross-border shopping</li>
        <li>Snowbird travel (Canadians spending winters in the US)</li>
        <li>Freelance and contractor payments</li>
        <li>Supply chains that cross the border more than once before a product is finished</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What makes this pair a little different</h2>
      <p>
        Interest rates matter here too — the <a href="https://www.bankofcanada.ca" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bank of Canada</a> versus the Federal Reserve. But the Canadian dollar
        also has a real link to oil. Canada exports a lot of crude oil, and the loonie has tended to move with
        oil prices over time. That means USD/CAD can shift on energy news that has nothing to do with either
        country's interest rate policy. Canada's other resource exports — minerals, timber — play a smaller,
        similar role.
      </p>

      <h2>Tips for cross-border shopping and travel</h2>
      <p>
        Many US and Canadian stores show prices in both currencies, or let you pick your billing currency at
        checkout. When you get that choice, pick the merchant's local currency and let your own card handle
        the conversion — letting the merchant convert for you (called dynamic currency conversion) usually
        means a worse rate. If you cross the border often, a card with no foreign transaction fees saves more
        over time than chasing small daily rate swings.
      </p>

      <h2>How trade policy shapes this relationship</h2>
      <p>
        The US and Canada have run under free trade agreements for decades — the original Canada-US Free
        Trade Agreement, then NAFTA starting in 1994, then USMCA since 2020. These deals don't set the
        exchange rate directly. But they enable so much trade that tariff disputes or renegotiation talks can
        still shake the currency, as markets try to price in the impact. Car manufacturing is especially
        sensitive here — supply chains cross the border multiple times before a vehicle is finished.
      </p>

      <h2>Common mistakes with this pair</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Mixing up the dollar sign:</strong> USD and CAD both use "$". Always check which one a Canadian retailer's price is actually in before you buy — they aren't interchangeable.</li>
        <li><strong>Assuming the two currencies move together:</strong> the economies are closely linked, but the loonie's oil sensitivity means it can diverge from what interest rates alone would suggest, especially during energy price swings.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Provincial and seasonal patterns worth knowing</h2>
      <p>
        Canada's economy isn't the same everywhere. Oil-producing provinces like Alberta feel energy price
        swings much more than service-heavy provinces like Ontario or British Columbia — which is part of why
        Canadian monetary policy can be debated internally. There's also a loose "snowbird season" pattern,
        when Canadians heading south for winter convert currency for extended stays — though this effect is
        small next to interest rates and oil prices. Treat seasonal patterns as background, not a signal to
        trade on: a single rate decision or oil price move can easily overwhelm them in any given week.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FAQAccordion
    faqs={pairFaqs}
    id="usd-cad-faq"
    eyebrow="USD/CAD FAQ"
    heading="Common USD to CAD questions"
    description="Quick answers before you convert."
  />

  <RelatedPairs
    links={[
      { label: 'USD to AUD', href: '/usd-to-aud' },
      { label: 'USD to EUR', href: '/usd-to-eur' },
      { label: 'USD to GBP', href: '/usd-to-gbp' },
      { label: 'GBP to USD', href: '/gbp-to-usd' },
    ]}
  />

  <script>
    import '../scripts/converter.js';
  </script>
</Layout>

<style>
  .editorial-body { max-width: 780px; }
</style>
